Hi,

I seem to be hitting an odd scenario here, maybe somebody can clarify or
try themselves and see if they can reproduce it.

I've created a raid0 device (/dev/md0).  I then pvcreate, vgcreate and
create an lv on the device (/dev/foo/foo).  After rebooting the machine
I startup the raid and lvm.  Here's the fun part.

If I run vgchange -ay foo to activate the volume, all is fine it seems.
If I now run vgscan I can no longer access foo.  Running vgchange -ay or
vgchange -an returns:

[root@dhcp8 tools]# ./vgchange -an foo
vgchange -- ERROR: VGDA in kernel and lvmtab are NOT consistent; please
run vgscan

And so I run vgscan... <endless loop goes here> :)

I first noticed this when I had vgscan in rc.sysinit and it ran first,
same thing happened, so I disabled the rc.sysinit startup and tried
skipping vgscan to see what happened.

I get the feeling this has something todo with MD devices only, it seems
to detect my other volume "vol" which I haven't been accessing while
testing out 0.9.1 the whole time.  It was created with 0.8final.  Possibly
something related with the recent patch I saw submitted that allows 0.9.1
to operate with MD?

I've attached the -d log for vgscan output, vgchange doesn't produce one.

